WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.990
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:08.537
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.837 --> 00:00:17.016
Unit fifty-one: Ageing, Death and Meaning. In this unit, you will reflect respectfully on mortality, memory and meaning.

4
00:00:18.017 --> 00:00:19.320
mortality.

5
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:24.411
mortality means the fact that every living being must die.

6
00:00:24.711 --> 00:00:28.162
Facing mortality can make life feel more precious.

7
00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:32.338
Your turn. Say: mortality.

8
00:00:35.038 --> 00:00:36.132
mortality.

9
00:00:37.117 --> 00:00:38.118
end of life.

10
00:00:38.818 --> 00:00:42.094
end of life means the final period of a person's life.

11
00:00:42.394 --> 00:00:45.578
Good end of life care respects the patient's wishes.

12
00:00:46.278 --> 00:00:48.977
Your turn. Say: end of life.

13
00:00:51.677 --> 00:00:52.562
end of life.

14
00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:54.621
bereavement.

15
00:00:55.321 --> 00:00:59.515
bereavement means the experience of losing someone close through death.

16
00:00:59.815 --> 00:01:02.662
Bereavement affects the body as well as the mind.

17
00:01:03.362 --> 00:01:06.188
Your turn. Say: bereavement.

18
00:01:08.888 --> 00:01:09.854
bereavement.

19
00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:12.072
mourning ritual.

20
00:01:12.772 --> 00:01:16.513
mourning ritual means traditional practices that help people grieve.

21
00:01:16.813 --> 00:01:19.799
White clothing is part of the Khmer mourning ritual.

22
00:01:20.499 --> 00:01:23.035
Your turn. Say: mourning ritual.

23
00:01:25.735 --> 00:01:26.864
mourning ritual.

24
00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:28.956
good death.

25
00:01:29.656 --> 00:01:34.465
good death means dying peacefully, without pain, surrounded by loved ones.

26
00:01:34.765 --> 00:01:36.869
Many hope for a good death at home.

27
00:01:37.569 --> 00:01:40.186
Your turn. Say: good death.

28
00:01:42.886 --> 00:01:43.818
good death.

29
00:01:44.817 --> 00:01:46.247
ancestral memory.

30
00:01:46.947 --> 00:01:52.140
ancestral memory means the remembrance of ancestors kept alive by families and rituals.

31
00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.472
Pchum Ben keeps ancestral memory alive.

32
00:01:56.172 --> 00:01:59.382
Your turn. Say: ancestral memory.

33
00:02:02.082 --> 00:02:03.617
ancestral memory.

34
00:02:04.617 --> 00:02:05.769
life review.

35
00:02:06.469 --> 00:02:10.628
life review means looking back over one's life to find meaning and peace.

36
00:02:10.928 --> 00:02:14.471
In life review, elders tell their stories to the young.

37
00:02:15.171 --> 00:02:18.381
Your turn. Say: life review.

38
00:02:21.081 --> 00:02:22.221
life review.

39
00:02:23.217 --> 00:02:24.566
existential meaning.

40
00:02:25.266 --> 00:02:29.355
existential meaning means a sense of purpose that makes life feel worthwhile.

41
00:02:29.655 --> 00:02:33.501
Existential meaning often comes from love and service.

42
00:02:34.201 --> 00:02:37.503
Your turn. Say: existential meaning.

43
00:02:40.203 --> 00:02:41.646
existential meaning.

44
00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:44.812
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:45.112 --> 00:02:49.596
Ms. Sarah, why do so many cultures avoid talking about death?

46
00:02:50.883 --> 00:02:56.450
Why indeed? Perhaps because to speak of death is to admit our own mortality.

47
00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:00.017
But Pchum Ben is all about the dead.

48
00:03:01.317 --> 00:03:06.790
Exactly. It honours the dead, comforts the living and connects the generations.

49
00:03:08.083 --> 00:03:11.790
In the West, death often happens in hospitals.

50
00:03:13.083 --> 00:03:17.953
Yes. We have added years to life, but have we added life to years?

51
00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:22.341
So a good death matters as much as a long life.

52
00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:27.321
Not the length of a life, but its depth, is what we remember.

53
00:03:28.617 --> 00:03:33.089
Grammar focus: Rhetorical devices in argument (advanced).

54
00:03:33.389 --> 00:03:37.061
Rhetorical devices give arguments emotional force.

55
00:03:37.361 --> 00:03:42.228
Pchum Ben honours the dead, comforts the living and connects the generations.

56
00:03:42.528 --> 00:03:46.176
We have added years to life, but have we added life to years?

57
00:03:46.476 --> 00:03:53.630
Chiasmus (A-B, B-A) creates memorable contrasts; use it once at a climax, not repeatedly.

58
00:03:54.617 --> 00:03:57.768
Now read and listen. Learning to Speak of Death.

59
00:03:58.068 --> 00:04:02.041
Every human culture must find a way to face mortality.

60
00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:10.539
In many modern societies, however, death has moved from the home to the hospital and out of everyday conversation.

61
00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:18.087
Medical progress has extended life, but it has also raised difficult questions about end of life care.

62
00:04:19.383 --> 00:04:27.525
Hospice movements argue for a good death: free from pain, surrounded by loved ones and in accordance with one's wishes.

63
00:04:28.817 --> 00:04:36.099
Mourning rituals, such as Cambodian funerals and the festival of Pchum Ben, help families through bereavement.

64
00:04:37.383 --> 00:04:42.239
These rituals also preserve ancestral memory, linking the living with the dead.

65
00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:50.205
Psychologists note that life review allows older people to find existential meaning in their experiences.

66
00:04:51.517 --> 00:04:57.208
To speak honestly of death, paradoxically, may be one of the best ways to live fully.

67
00:04:58.517 --> 00:05:01.239
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:01.539 --> 00:05:04.421
The experience of losing someone close is blank.

69
00:05:04.721 --> 00:05:09.187
A, bereavement. B, mortality. C, life review.

70
00:05:13.183 --> 00:05:16.193
The answer is A: bereavement.

71
00:05:17.683 --> 00:05:21.320
'Added years to life, but life to years' is an example of blank.

72
00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:26.214
A, chiasmus. B, ellipsis. C, passive voice.

73
00:05:30.217 --> 00:05:32.959
The answer is A: chiasmus.

74
00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:35.811
Which is a tricolon?

75
00:05:36.111 --> 00:05:43.712
A, honours the dead, comforts the living and connects the generations. B, honours the dead. C, the living.

76
00:05:47.717 --> 00:05:53.188
The answer is A: honours the dead, comforts the living and connects the generations.

77
00:05:54.683 --> 00:05:57.960
Looking back over one's life to find meaning is blank.

78
00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:03.446
A, life review. B, mourning ritual. C, good death.

79
00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:10.239
The answer is A: life review.

80
00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:15.712
According to the text, what do rituals such as Pchum Ben preserve?

81
00:06:16.012 --> 00:06:21.778
A, ancestral memory. B, medical records. C, family wealth.

82
00:06:25.783 --> 00:06:28.503
The answer is A: ancestral memory.

83
00:06:30.017 --> 00:06:35.664
Now it's your turn. Part one: How can families support elderly relatives at the end of life?

84
00:06:40.964 --> 00:06:47.480
Part two: Use a rhetorical question and a tricolon; speak with sensitivity.

85
00:06:53.780 --> 00:07:04.367
What do our elders need most? Not expensive treatment alone, but presence. We should listen to their stories, respect their wishes and keep them close. That is how we honour a life.

86
00:07:04.667 --> 00:07:06.841
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:13.335
Great work today! You learned eight new words and rhetorical devices in argument (advanced).

88
00:07:13.635 --> 00:07:18.041
Today's value is togetherness. Spend happy time with your family.

89
00:07:18.341 --> 00:07:21.051
See you in Unit fifty-two. Goodbye!

90
00:07:21.351 --> 00:07:24.294
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

